[size=5][b]⊹ Installation[/b][/size]

[list]
[*]Requires OpenMW 0.49 or newer.
[*]Install and enable [url=https://www.nexusmods.com/morrowind/mods/60256]Max Yari's Script Services (MSS)[/url], it's a required dependency.
[*]Install this mod with a mod organiser: download the archive (or this repository as an archive) and drag and drop it into your mod organiser of choice (e.g [url=https://github.com/ModOrganizer2/modorganizer/releases]Mod Organizer 2[/url] on Windows or [url=https://github.com/grazelandsnomad/nerevarine_organizer/releases/tag/v0.70]Nerevarine Organizer[/url] on Linux). Or [url=https://modding-openmw.com/tips/installing-mods/]read this tutorial[/url] on how to install mods using the launcher or completely manually (it's also very easy).
[*]Enable the mod's .omwscripts file in the "Content Files" tab of the OpenMW launcher.
[*]Disable vanilla crosshair in [b]Options->Prefs->Crosshair (at the bottom).[/b]
[*]Ensure that shaders are enabled in game settings (enemy HP widget is a shader).
[/list]

[size=5][b]⊹ Configuration[/b][/size]

Check [b]Options->Scripts->Dynamic Reticle[/b] - the mod is very configurable. You can even drop new reticle images into a supported folder (it's specified also there in options) and be able to select between them in script options.

[size=5][b]⊹ How To Remove Vanilla Enemy Health Bar[/b][/size]

If you are using the oblivion-style reticular health bar bundled with hits mod - you might want to disable original yellow enemy hp bar - this is how:

[list=1]
[*]Find [font=Courier New]./OpenMW/resources/vfs/mygui/openmw_hud.layout[/font] (inside your OpenMW folder, not your Morrowind folder!), if you are using a mod replacing/modifying vanilla hud, such as the beautiful [url=https://www.nexusmods.com/morrowind/mods/53267?tab=posts&BH=1]Centered HUD for OpenMW[/url] - look for [font=Courier New]/mygui/openmw_hud.layout[/font] inside the mod folder instead (if you are using a mod manager to install it)
[*]Open the file with a text editor
[*]Find an element with a name EnemyHealth ([font=Courier New]name="EnemyHealth"[/font])
[*]In the "position" property of that element - 3rd number is this element's length - set it to 0 to hide the element
[/list]

e.g you might find an element that looks like this

[code]<Widget type="ProgressBar" skin="MW_EnergyBar_Yellow" position="0 131 80 12" align="Center Bottom" name="EnemyHealth">
    <Property key="Visible" value="false"/>
</Widget>[/code]

inside position = "..." change 80 to 0. Save the file. Play the game.

